# gn4 indexer — change detection via system versioning, and the cron story

Status: design + validated queries. No gn4 code change yet; nothing
written to production. Owner approved the approach 2026-10-12
("Versioning sounds good to me! We can run production any way we want.").
This note covers objective 5 (versioning delta) and objective 6 (cron).

## The problem

The native Rust indexer rebuilds by hashing every document's full source.
That is correct but pays the whole crawl even when almost nothing changed.
The goal is to narrow the work to the changed rows, without ever letting
the fast path be the thing that proves the index correct.

## Design 1 — change detection (delta)

One-time `ALTER TABLE <t> ADD SYSTEM VERSIONING;` on the production
tables -- one command per table, run on the production side. MariaDB then exposes `ROW_START`/`ROW_END`
(`TIMESTAMP(6)`), read with plain `SELECT`. No binlog, no extra
privileges beyond SELECT.

* Coverage marker: the run's DB `NOW(6)`, stored in the sidecar manifest.
  The manifest is written last, after the index commit, so the marker never
  advances ahead of the index it describes; a crash routes to a full run.
* Window invariant: capture the marker before querying, advance it after
  committing. Query `[prev_marker, new_marker)`.
* Late-commit hazard: a transaction that starts before the marker but
  commits after the scan has `ROW_START` inside the window and is
  invisible to it. A naive advance would then skip it forever. Mitigation:
  re-query an overlap window `[new_marker - O, now)` each run (idempotent),
  plus the periodic verification sweep as the bounded backstop.
* Deletes: a `DELETE` only moves `ROW_END`, so the window must test
  `ROW_END` as well as `ROW_START`. Liveness is decided by current
  existence, not by `ROW_END` alone.
* Contributing tables that are not versioned (cache text, `Geno`,
  `Phenotype`, `Publication`, …) are invisible to the delta and are caught
  only by the sweep — stated as a boundary, not an edge case.
* Purge detection: if the oldest resolvable history is newer than the
  marker, fall back to a full run.

### The safety rule (why this is safe)

The delta only ever narrows which documents the full sweep rebuilds. The
correctness proof remains a periodic **full source-hash sweep**. A delta
miss is corrected within one sweep period.

## Design 2 — cron story

Two schedules, one lock:

* Delta run every 10 minutes (low-latency DB freshness).
* Verification sweep every 3 hours (the correctness bound; catches
  cache-text edits and unversioned tables).

Overlap protection is a single `flock` on a held file descriptor, with
skip-and-log on a busy lock. `flock` is crash-safe (the kernel releases it
on `kill -9`); a pidfile is not. Queueing is rejected — a long sweep would
build a backlog faster than it drains.

Lag SLA is measured on the DB clock: how far the manifest marker trails
`NOW(6)`. The alert threshold is set relative to the worst-case run
duration (production sweep is ~23–99 min), not the tick period, so a
healthy long sweep does not page.

## Validated (local MariaDB, throwaway versioned copies)

* no change → empty delta, writes nothing; change → exactly the changed
  row; delete → `ROW_END`; insert-then-delete → no document.
* late-commit reproduced: naive advance misses permanently; overlap window
  catches it.
* marker sabotage (NULL/future/absent) → fall back to full.
* history purge → fall back to full.
* invisible change (cache-text edit): delta 0 rows, sweep rewrites the
  documents, result equals a fresh full build (46,732 matched, 0
  mismatched).
* flock: second holder skips; after `kill -9` the next one acquires.

## Open questions

* How often are full index rebuilds expected in normal operation? This
  prices the remaining speed work.
* When to schedule the one-time system-versioning `ALTER` on the
  production tables? It is one command per table, and the rest of the
  design waits on nothing else.
* Worst-case transaction duration on the write path (to size the overlap
  window `O`); until measured, a conservative constant plus the sweep
  backstop is used.

## Not in scope

Speed and memory of the delta query at production scale (indexed access to
the generated columns) are unmeasured; correctness does not depend on it.
